## Statement

Let $q=e^{\pi i/\ell}$, $\ell\ge3$, $\delta=q+q^{-1}$, $m=\ell-1$ and $f=p_m$. Put $B_n=\mathrm{TL}_n(\mathbb C,\delta)$ and

$$J_n=\begin{cases}0,&n<m,\\ B_n(f\otimes1_{n-m})B_n,&n\ge m.\end{cases}$$

If $\mathcal I$ is the categorical tensor ideal generated by $f$, then $\mathcal I(n,n)=J_n$ at every rank. Moreover, for $n\ge1$, $\operatorname{ptr}_n(J_n)\subseteq J_{n-1}$. Thus this single embedded two-sided ideal already contains every annular consequence with arbitrary external strands and cups/caps. Below the boundary its component is zero, including contractions given by a partial trace rather than an internal turnback.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The complex diagram category and the exact boundary projector.

[F1] [[def-temperley-lieb-diagram-category]], [[thm-temperley-lieb-diagram-generators-span]] and [[lem-temperley-lieb-generators-satisfy-local-relations]] give the matching basis, cup/cap snakes and generator relations.

[F2] [[def-temperley-lieb-closure-trace-and-partial-trace]] and [[lem-temperley-lieb-closure-trace-component-identities]] give unnormalized partial closure and its composition rules.

[F3] [[lem-temperley-lieb-jones-wenzl-partial-trace-and-uniqueness]] gives $U_if=fU_i=0$, its one-dimensional corner and $\operatorname{ptr}_m(f)=([m+1]/[m])p_{m-1}$.

[F4] [[def-temperley-lieb-quantum-integers-and-quantum-characteristic]] gives $[\ell]=0$, $[j]>0$ for $1\le j<\ell$, and $\delta>0$.

## Proof

1.1 For $n\ge1$ write $E_n=\operatorname{ptr}_n$. Closing matching graphs gives $B_{n-1}$-bilinearity and $E_n(x\otimes1)=\delta x$. For $n\ge2$, it also gives $E_n(U_{n-1})=1$. In this latter range, for $x\in B_{n-1}$, $U_{n-1}(x\otimes1)U_{n-1}=(E_{n-1}(x)\otimes1\otimes1)U_{n-1}.$ Indeed the two neighboring cups/caps close precisely the last input/output strand of $x$, leaving its other strands and the final cup/cap unchanged; every closed component contributes the same factor on both sides. For $x=1$ this gives $U_{n-1}^2=\delta U_{n-1}$, fixing the unnormalized factor. Since $B_n$ is generated by $B_{n-1}$ and $U_{n-1}$, this identity reduces every alternating word with two final generators to one with fewer such occurrences. Hence $B_n$ is spanned by $B_{n-1}+B_{n-1}U_{n-1}B_{n-1}$. [F1, F2, algebra]

1.2 To handle arbitrary-size morphisms we give an auxiliary diagram factorization. It does not identify a positive-loop pivotal category with the quantum ribbon category. Choose $A=i e^{\pi i/(2\ell)}$, so $\delta=-A^2-A^{-2}$, and put $C=A\,1+A^{-1}U$. Its inverse is $A^{-1}1+AU$ because $A^2+A^{-2}+\delta=0$. The adjacent triple expansion gives Yang–Baxter, with remaining coefficient $A+A^{-1}\delta+A^{-3}=0$. Let $W_1=\cup\otimes1$ and $W_2=1\otimes\cup$. The snakes give $U_1W_1=\delta W_1$, $U_2W_1=W_2$ and $U_1W_2=W_1$, whence $C_1C_2W_1=(A^2+\delta+A^{-2})W_1+W_2=W_2,\qquad C_2C_1W_2=W_1.$ Reflection gives the two corresponding cap slides. Thus ordered block crossings $C_{r,t}:r+t\to t+r$ are natural with every matching, since cups, caps and identities generate those morphisms; linearity gives naturality with arbitrary diagram elements. Partitioning a block proves both block-crossing identities, and the triple relation identifies the ordered crossing words. All crossings used here are finite linear combinations of ordinary diagrams. [F1, F4, algebra, construct]

2.1 Every nonempty generator word kills $f$ on its appropriate side, so $J_m=\mathbb Cf$. The boundary partial trace is $E_m(f)=\frac{[\ell]}{[\ell-1]}p_{\ell-2}=0.$ Its denominator is nonzero by [F4]. Thus $E_m(J_m)=0=J_{m-1}$. This covers a seam contraction even when no two adjacent input endpoints of the projector are joined by a cap. [F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 Denote by $\vartheta_t$ the planar curl of this auxiliary block crossing. Closing one strand gives $\vartheta_1=A\delta+A^{-1}=-A^3$. The cup/cap slides of step 1.2 give $\vartheta_{r+t}=C_{t,r}C_{r,t}(\vartheta_r\otimes\vartheta_t),\qquad \operatorname{ptr}_{t}(C_{t,t})=\vartheta_t,$ where $\operatorname{ptr}_t$ closes the last $t$ strands. To verify the second identity, close those strands from right to left, slide each cap through its block crossing by the displayed cap equations, then remove its matching cup by a snake. One remaining strand acquires the scalar curl $-A^3$; the unclosed strands retain the two block-crossing words in the first identity. Induction, beginning with the computed one-strand value, proves both formulas. In particular $\vartheta_t$ is $(-A^3)^t$ times a product of invertible crossings, so its inverse exists in $B_t$. This is a calculation inside the positive-loop diagram category, independent of any universal quantum $R$. [F1, F2, step 1.2, induction, algebra]

3.1 Inclusion gives $J_n\otimes1\subseteq J_{n+1}$. We prove $E_n(J_n)\subseteq J_{n-1}$ by induction; below $m$ it is trivial and at $m$ it is step 2.1. Let $n>m$, so the embedded generator is in $B_{n-1}$. Expand each multiplier of a generator $afb$ by the spanning formula of step 1.1. With all $a_i,b_i\in B_{n-1}$ and $U=U_{n-1}$, the four types are $a_0fb_0$, $a_1Ua_2fb_0$, $a_0fb_1Ub_2$, and $a_1Ua_2fb_1Ub_2$. Their partial traces are, respectively, $\delta a_0fb_0,\qquad a_1a_2fb_0,\qquad a_0fb_1b_2,\qquad a_1E_{n-1}(a_2fb_1)b_2.$ The first three belong to $J_{n-1}$. In the fourth, the induction hypothesis puts the middle factor in $J_{n-2}$, whose inclusion belongs to $J_{n-1}$, or is zero at the boundary. This proves trace closure. Iterated partial traces consequently carry $J_N$ into $J_n$ for every $n\le N$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, induction, algebra]

4.1 Fix arbitrary $a:t\to n$, $b:n\to t$ and $F=f\otimes1_{t-m}$, with $t\ge m$. Set $N=n+t$ and form the actual element $X=(a\otimes b)(F\vartheta_t^{-1}\otimes1_n)C_{n,t}\in B_N.$ The tensor $a\otimes b$ is an endomorphism of object $N$: its source has $t+n$ strands and its target $n+t$ strands. Its middle factor equals $(f\otimes1_{N-m})(\vartheta_t^{-1}\otimes1_n)$, so $X\in J_N$. Naturality gives $(1_t\otimes b)C_{n,t}=C_{t,t}(b\otimes1_t)$, so also $X=(aF\vartheta_t^{-1}\otimes1_t)C_{t,t}(b\otimes1_t)$. Pulling maps on the untraced wires outside the closure and using step 2.2 now gives $\operatorname{ptr}_t(X)=aF\vartheta_t^{-1}\operatorname{ptr}_t(C_{t,t})b=aFb.$ By step 3.1 this belongs to $J_n$, even when $n<m$, where it is zero. This is an explicit factorization for all external strands and all seam contractions, not a claim that every low-rank diagram has an internal cap against $f$. [F1, F2, step 3.1, step 1.2, step 2.2, algebra]

5.1 A tensor-ideal word containing $f$ is a finite sum of composites $a(1_r\otimes f\otimes1_u)b$. If it contains further copies of $f$, expand those in their finite matching basis and absorb them into the multipliers, leaving one distinguished copy. Naturality with the auxiliary crossings moves $1_r\otimes f\otimes1_u$ to $f\otimes1_{r+u}$ by an invertible conjugation, absorbed in $a,b$. Step 4.1 therefore puts every such consequence in $J_n$, proving $\mathcal I(n,n)\subseteq J_n$. Conversely tensoring $f$ with identities and composing on either side puts the generators and all their two-sided multiples in $\mathcal I$. Hence $J_n\subseteq\mathcal I(n,n)$. Their equality and all partial-trace assertions follow, including the zero components below $m$. [step 3.1, step 1.2, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
